\n\nAlternatively, **N: b8-c6** develops a piece while attacking both knights, but the d7-d6 pawn move is more forcing and creates immediate problems for White's overextended position.\n\nReason: P: d7-d6 attacks White's advanced e5 knight, forcing it to retreat or be exchanged, while opening lines for my pieces and maintaining central control. This is the most direct way to challenge White's aggressive but potentially overextended setup.\n\nAnswer: P: d7-d6"}
